syntax: parse zsh's anonymous function arguments and function forms

Zsh runs an anonymous function right away and passes it the words which
follow its body, it declares several functions at once without the
function keyword, and it allows a separator between the names and the
body (zshmisc(1), "SHELL GRAMMAR" and "ALTERNATE FORMS FOR COMPLEX
COMMANDS"):

	() { echo $1 } arg1 arg2
	function { echo $1 } arg
	start stop () { echo $0 }
	function start stop; echo $0
	echo $(() { echo $1 } arg)

Reproducer, before this change:

	$ printf '() { echo $1 } arg\n' | shfmt -ln zsh
	<standard input>:1:16: statements must be separated by &, ; or a newline
	$ printf 'start stop () { echo $0 }\n' | shfmt -ln zsh
	<standard input>:1:12: a command can only contain words and redirects; encountered `(`
	$ printf 'echo $(() { echo hi })\n' | shfmt -ln zsh
	<standard input>:1:6: `$((` must be followed by an expression

FuncDecl gains an Args field for the arguments of an anonymous function,
a list of literal words followed by `()` now declares a function with
Names, and `$((` or `((` followed by `)` and another character is lexed
as the start of an anonymous function rather than of arithmetic. The
printer writes a semicolon between `function` names and a body which
starts with a word, which would otherwise read as one more name.

Part of https://github.com/mvdan/sh/issues/120.

